note:
These are taken from real calls in Home transcripts, with values blanked. Each path keeps only its first segment, and <id> stands for the rest.

db_write. ops is a list. One entry holds op, path and data, plus ifVersion when the document already exists. There is no separate collection key: the collection is the first segment of path. Paths seen: items/<id>, receipts/<id>, groups/<id>, meta/<id>.
{"slug": "", "ops": [{"op": "set", "path": "receipts/<id>", "data": {"read": ""}}]}
{"slug": "", "ops": [{"op": "update", "path": "receipts/<id>", "ifVersion": 1, "data": {"filed": ""}}]}

The op values are set, merge, update and delete. data can hold {"__delete__": true} to remove a field.

db_str_replace. path is always items/<id> in the calls found. field names one top-level field, such as context.
{"slug": "", "path": "items/<id>", "field": "context", "old_str": "", "new_str": "", "ifVersion": 2}
db_get. path is <collection>/<id>. Forms seen: answers/<id>, items/<id>, receipts/<id>, meta/<id> (for example meta/page).
{"slug": "", "path": "answers/<id>"}
publish_files. files is a list, not a map. Each entry is {as, path}; the schema also allows uri, which the client fills in.
{"files": [{"as": "index.html", "path": "<abs path>"}], "title": "", "icon": ""}
{"slug": "", "base_version": "", "label": "", "files": [{"as": "", "path": "<abs path>"}]}

Two other tools take a collection rather than a path: db_query (collection, where, limit, orderBy) and db_list (collection, limit). collection is a bare name such as items.
